The Australian film industry is currently in a dire strait - many films get made but they struggle to be noticed (by the media or audiences) and often disappear without trace.

&#x200B;

The current state of the film industry is reflected in the misfortunes of Metro magazine - a film journal that has been going for nearly 60 years but is apparently about to call it quits. Metro was famed for supporting the local film industry,and regularly covered  every film being made.  But few people are interested in reading about Australians films, let alone watching them. Here was one appeal to people to help raise funds which explains the way things currently stand (or fall).   

&#x200B;

"Metro would ideally remain an integral part of a fragile ecosystem: supporting the magazine is really (or also) supporting the hard work of typically unheralded and endangered filmmakers similarly trying to survive in an increasingly hostile environment (the global marketplace). Metro acknowledges and legitimates Australian cinema in a culture intent on marginalising or displacing its own voices and/or media. Metro needs to be supported so it can continue to support other people's contribution to Australian cinema. Metro acts as a corrective to a disposable consumer culture by being a repository of film reportage and criticism. Specifically, Metro offers an invaluable historical document of films that would otherwise fall by the wayside or disappear without a trace. It thereby seeks to acknowledge and/or preserve their cultural significance (value, import) by entering into an ongoing dialogue with uniquely Australian voices and talent".

&#x200B;

https://www.gofundme.com/f/help-save-australias-oldest-film-publication
